{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\noutput_path = Path('/Users/chrgre01/src/Sapi/verification/reasoning_quality/run-20260426T075611Z/high/site/spaces/alpha/runs/run-20260426T080526Z--4fls3f47jk/semantic/ingest_extraction.json')\noutput_path.parent.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)\n\ndata = {\n  \\\"source_date_inference\\\": {\n    \\\"confidence\\\": \\\"partial\\\",\n    \\\"date\\\": None,\n    \\\"origin\\\": \\\"source_markdown_and_task_context\\\",\n    \\\"rationale\\\": \\\"The markdown identifies Pacific Philosophical Quarterly 94 (2013) and a 2013 copyright line, but the task context reports no resolved publication date; no exact date is inferred.\\\"\n  },\n  \\\"source\\\": {\n    \\\"source_id\\\": \\\"source-grounding-necessary-or-contingent--6e8195a096f0\\\",\n    \\\"display_title\\\": \\\"Grounding: Necessary or Contingent\\\",\n    \\\"authors\\\": [\n      \\\"Kelly Trogdon\\\"\n    ],\n    \\\"venue\\\": \\\"Pacific Philosophical Quarterly\\\",\n    \\\"visible_year\\\": 2013,\n    \\\"doi\\\": \\\"10.1111/papq.12009\\\"\n  },\n  \\\"claims\\\": [\n    {\n      \\\"claim_id\\\": \\\"claim-full-grounding-necessitates-grounded-proposition\\\",\n      \\\"short_title\\\": \\\"Full grounds necessitate consequents\\\",\n      \\\"text\\\": \\\"If a fact [p] is fully grounded in a plurality of facts D, then it is metaphysically necessary that the conjunction of propositions corresponding to D entails p.\\\",\n      \\\"evidence_excerpts\\\": [\n        \\\"necessity: if [p] is (fully) grounded in D, then ☐(∧D → p).\\\",\n        \\\"(1) Suppose that [p] is (fully) grounded in D. ... (5) By (3) and (4), ☐(∧D → p).\\\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \\\"claim_id\\\": \\\"claim-grounding-distinction-is-explanatory\\\",\n      \\\"short_title\\\": \\\"Grounding exceeds modal entailment\\\",\n      \\\"text\\\": \\\"The distinction between full and merely partial grounding is an explanatory distinction rather than a purely modal distinction.\\\",\n      \\\"evidence_excerpts\\\": [\n        \\\"Moral: the distinction between full and mere partial grounding is an explanatory distinction rather than a purely modal one.\\\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \\\"claim_id\\\": \\\"claim-significance-constrains-grounding\\\",\n      \\\"short_title\\\": \\\"Grounding excludes significant questions\\\",\n      \\\"text\\\": \\\"Full grounding obtains only when the relevant connecting question lacks cognitive significance.\\\",\n      \\\"evidence_excerpts\\\": [\n        \\\"significance: [p] is (fully) grounded in D only if the connecting questions with respect to [p] and D lack cognitive significance.\\\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \\\"claim_id\\\": \\\"claim-cognitive-significance-requires-no-accessible-answer\\\",\n      \\\"short_title\\\": \\\"Significance lacks accessible answers\\\",\n      \\\"text\\\": \\\"A connecting question has cognitive significance only when full information about the relevant facts and entities would still not make the question lose substantive content for rational subjects.\\\",\n      \\\"evidence_excerpts\\\": [\n        \\\"Let a connecting question of the form ‘Why should [p] obtain given that each of the members of D obtains?’ be cognitively significant just in case it’s metaphysically necessary that, for any rational individual, being fully informed about the nature of [p], D, the plurality consisting of [p] and D, and the entities these facts involve doesn’t suffice for the question to lack substantive content for that individual.\\\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \\\"claim_id\\\": \\\"claim-essence-closes-connecting-questions\\\",\n      \\\"short_title\\\": \\\"Essence explains question closure\\\",\n      \\\"text\\\": \\\"When a connecting question lacks cognitive significance, relevant Fine-style essential truths explain why the grounds entail the grounded proposition.\\\",\n      \\\"evidence_excerpts\\\": [\n        \\\"essence: the connecting questions with respect to [p] and D lack cognitive significance only if there are certain essential truths characteristic of [p], D, the plurality consisting of [p] and D, or the entities they involve according to which ∧D → p.\\\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \\\"claim_id\\\": \\\"claim-essential-truths-ground-necessity\\\",\n      \\\"short_title\\\": \\\"Essence entails modal necessity\\\",\n      \\\"text\\\": \\\"Fine-style essential truths are metaphysically necessary, so essential truths of the form ∧D → p support the modal necessity of grounding entailment.\\\",\n      \\\"evidence_excerpts\\\": [\n        \\\"If p is an essential truth with respect to entity e, then p is true regardless of what the world might be like; the truth of p doesn’t turn on how things turn out.\\\",\n        \\\"The essential truths are metaphysically necessary.\\\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \\\"claim_id\\\": \\\"claim-paradigms-support-significance\\\",\n      \\\"short_title\\\": \\\"Paradigms support epistemic constraint\\\",\n      \\\"text\\\": \\\"Paradigmatic grounding cases support the significance principle because their connecting questions lack substantive content even for minimally informed subjects.\\\",\n      \\\"evidence_excerpts\\\": [\n        \\\"paradigm cases of grounding have the relevant epistemic feature, two of which we’ve already discussed: the grounding of [p∧q] in [p], [q], and the grounding of [p⁄q] in [p]\\\",\n        \\\"the connecting question, ‘Why should [p⁄q] obtain given that [p] obtains?’ certainly lacks cognitive significance\\\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \\\"claim_id\\\": \\\"claim-counterexamples-misidentify-grounds\\\",\n      \\\"short_title\\\": \\\"Counterexamples miss full grounds\\\",\n      \\\"text\\\": \\\"Putative counterexamples involving promises, universal generalizations, and widowhood fail when merely partial grounds are supplemented by additional explanatorily relevant facts that restore modal entailment.\\\",\n      \\\"evidence_excerpts\\\": [\n        \\\"If such additional facts are available, we have reason to believe that D is a mere partial ground that was misidentified as a full ground; in such a case, [p] instead is fully grounded in D, P.\\\",\n        \\\"additional explanatorily relevant facts are available that meet the modal entailment condition: what are identified as enabling conditions in the first case, a totality fact ... in the second case, and the fact that Socrates and Xanthippe were married in the third case.\\\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \\\"claim_id\\\": \\\"claim-rose-existence-is-nonground\\\",\n      \\\"short_title\\\": \\\"Rose existence is nonground\\\",\n      \\\"text\\\": \\\"The rose trope objection fails because the existence of a particular redness is not grounded in the rose’s existence but in the rose’s being a particular shade of red.\\\",\n      \\\"evidence_excerpts\\\": [\n        \\\"the existence of the particular redness isn’t even partially grounded in the existence of the rose\\\",\n        \\\"the existence of the particular redness is fully grounded in the fact that the rose is a particular shade of red\\\"\n      ]\n    }\n  ],\n  \\\"evidence_items\\\": [\n    {\n      \\\"evidence_id\\\": \\\"evidence-necessity-argument-outline--4f8c2a9d1e3b\\\",\n      \\\"title\\\": \\\"Necessity Argument Outline\\\",\n      \\\"excerpt\\\": \\\"necessity: if [p] is (fully) grounded in D, then ☐(∧D → p). With these two principles in place, the argument for necessity is as follows: (1) Suppose that [p] is (fully) grounded in D. (2) By (1) and significance, the connecting question ‘Why should [p] obtain given that each member of D obtains?’ lacks cognitive significance. (3) By (2) and essence, there are essential truths about [p], D, the plurality consisting of [p] and D, or the entities they involve according to which ∧D → p. (4) The essential truths are metaphysically necessary. (5) By (3) and (4), ☐(∧D → p).\\\",\n      \\\"overview\\\": \\\"This is the paper’s central formal argument. It connects full grounding to the absence of cognitive significance, then to essential truths, and finally to metaphysical necessity. Its scope is restricted to full grounds and to the proposition corresponding to what is grounded, not to stronger claims that grounding itself must recur in every possible world.\\\",\n      \\\"evidence_type\\\": \\\"formal_argument\\\",\n      \\\"claim_refs\\\": [\n        \\\"claim-full-grounding-necessitates-grounded-proposition\\\",\n        \\\"claim-significance-constrains-grounding\\\",\n        \\\"claim-essence-closes-connecting-questions\\\",\n        \\\"claim-essential-truths-ground-necessity\\\"\n      ],\n      \\\"source_id\\\": \\\"source-grounding-necessary-or-contingent--6e8195a096f0\\\",\n      \\\"page_refs\\\": [\n        \\\"pp.467-468\\\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \\\"evidence_id\\\": \\\"evidence-modal-distinction-problem--9c7e6d2f4a10\\\",\n      \\\"title\\\": \\\"Modal Distinction Problem\\\",\n      \\\"excerpt\\\": \\\"Problem: facts corresponding to necessary truths can have mere partial grounds. If [p] is merely partially grounded in D and <p> is necessarily true, it’s a trivial matter that ☐(∧D → p). Consider again [p∧q]. Suppose that <p> is a necessary truth, as is <q>. For some suitable p and q, [p∧q] is merely partially grounded in [p], despite the fact that <p∧q> is true in all possible worlds. Moral: the distinction between full and mere partial grounding is an explanatory distinction rather than a purely modal one.\\\",\n      \\\"overview\\\": \\\"This passage rejects a shortcut argument from modal entailment to full grounding. It matters because the paper’s positive argument cannot simply define full grounding by modal entailment; it must explain why full grounding necessitates without collapsing grounding into modality. The example uses necessary truths to show that entailment can accompany partial grounding trivially.\\\",\n      \\\"evidence_type\\\": \\\"formal_argument\\\",\n      \\\"claim_refs\\\": [\n        \\\"claim-grounding-distinction-is-explanatory\\\"\n      ],\n      \\\"source_id\\\": \\\"source-grounding-necessary-or-contingent--6e8195a096f0\\\",\n      \\\"page_refs\\\": [\n        \\\"p.467\\\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \\\"evidence_id\\\": \\\"evidence-cognitive-significance-definition--2a6b8f0c9d31\\\",\n      \\\"title\\\": \\\"Cognitive Significance Definition\\\",\n      \\\"excerpt\\\": \\\"Let a connecting question of the form ‘Why should [p] obtain given that each of the members of D obtains?’ be cognitively significant just in case it’s metaphysically necessary that, for any rational individual, being fully informed about the nature of [p], D, the plurality consisting of [p] and D, and the entities these facts involve doesn’t suffice for the question to lack substantive content for that individual. So a connecting question of this form lacks cognitive significance just in case it’s possible that being fully informed about the natures of the relevant entities suffices for the question to lack substantive content for some rational individual.\\\",\n      \\\"overview\\\": \\\"The definition fixes the epistemic notion used in the main argument. It distinguishes questions whose explanatory force survives ideal information from questions that would disappear once a rational agent grasped the relevant natures. This makes the later significance principle a general constraint on grounding rather than a claim about any actual person’s ignorance.\\\",\n      \\\"evidence_type\\\": \\\"formal_argument\\\",\n      \\\"claim_refs\\\": [\n        \\\"claim-cognitive-significance-requires-no-accessible-answer\\\",\n        \\\"claim-significance-constrains-grounding\\\"\n      ],\n      \\\"source_id\\\": \\\"source-grounding-necessary-or-contingent--6e8195a096f0\\\",\n      \\\"page_refs\\\": [\n        \\\"pp.469-470\\\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \\\"evidence_id\\\": \\\"evidence-significance-principle-defense--7b1d3e5f9a42\\\",\n      \\\"title\\\": \\\"Significance Principle Defense\\\",\n      \\\"excerpt\\\": \\\"significance: [p] is (fully) grounded in D only if the connecting questions with respect to [p] and D lack cognitive significance. According to significance, this broadly epistemic feature is characteristic of grounding in general. ... paradigm cases of grounding have the relevant epistemic feature, two of which we’ve already discussed: the grounding of [p∧q] in [p], [q], and the grounding of [p⁄q] in [p], for some suitable p and q. With respect to the latter, for example, the connecting question, ‘Why should [p⁄q] obtain given that [p] obtains?’ certainly lacks cognitive significance.\\\",\n      \\\"overview\\\": \\\"This evidence supports the bridge from grounding to epistemology. Trogdon motivates the principle by appeal to paradigmatic cases of grounding whose explanatory connection is transparent enough that the associated connecting question lacks substantive content. The defense is abductive rather than deductive: paradigms guide extension to less clear grounding cases.\\\",\n      \\\"evidence_type\\\": \\\"formal_argument\\\",\n      \\\"claim_refs\\\": [\n        \\\"claim-significance-constrains-grounding\\\",\n        \\\"claim-paradigms-support-significance\\\"\n      ],\n      \\\"source_id\\\": \\\"source-grounding-necessary-or-contingent--6e8195a096f0\\\",\n      \\\"page_refs\\\": [\n        \\\"pp.472-473\\\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \\\"evidence_id\\\": \\\"evidence-essence-principle-statement--bd6a0f4c8e92\\\",\n      \\\"title\\\": \\\"Essence Principle Statement\\\",\n      \\\"excerpt\\\": \\\"essence: the connecting questions with respect to [p] and D lack cognitive significance only if there are certain essential truths characteristic of [p], D, the plurality consisting of [p] and D, or the entities they involve according to which ∧D → p. ... It’s overwhelmingly plausible to think that it’s something about the nature of the relevant entities that forecloses cognitive significance. The general idea is that, once you know what it is to be the relevant facts and the entities they involve, such questions no longer comport with your epistemic situation; when you grasp the relevant essential truths, you thereby see why it should be that [p] obtains given that each member of D obtains.\\\",\n      \\\"overview\\\": \\\"This passage supplies the second bridge in the argument. The absence of a substantive connecting question is explained by the nature, or essence, of the relevant facts and entities. It also clarifies why the paper appeals to Finean essence rather than treating the epistemic condition as a brute feature of grounding claims.\\\",\n      \\\"evidence_type\\\": \\\"formal_argument\\\",\n      \\\"claim_refs\\\": [\n        \\\"claim-essence-closes-connecting-questions\\\",\n        \\\"claim-essential-truths-ground-necessity\\\"\n      ],\n      \\\"source_id\\\": \\\"source-grounding-necessary-or-contingent--6e8195a096f0\\\",\n      \\\"page_refs\\\": [\n        \\\"pp.473-475\\\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \\\"evidence_id\\\": \\\"evidence-finean-essence-necessity--c0d5e7a1b943\\\",\n      \\\"title\\\": \\\"Finean Essence Necessity\\\",\n      \\\"excerpt\\\": \\\"Fine’s (1994) notion of essence is primitive, and he uses the notion of a ‘real’ as opposed to ‘nominal’ definition to communicate the notion he has mind. The basic idea is that, while a nominal definition says what a term means, a real definition tells us what an entity is. ... If p is an essential truth with respect to entity e, then p is true regardless of what the world might be like; the truth of p doesn’t turn on how things turn out. Cast in terms of grounding, the idea is that the essential truths lack worldly grounds.\\\",\n      \\\"overview\\\": \\\"This evidence explains why essential truths can underwrite metaphysical necessity in the main argument. By adopting Fine’s real-definition conception, the paper treats essential truths as not depending on contingent worldly arrangements. That makes the transition from an essential truth of the form ∧D → p to □(∧D → p) intelligible.\\\",\n      \\\"evidence_type\\\": \\\"formal_argument\\\",\n      \\\"claim_refs\\\": [\n        \\\"claim-essential-truths-ground-necessity\\\",\n        \\\"claim-essence-closes-connecting-questions\\\"\n      ],\n      \\\"source_id\\\": \\\"source-grounding-necessary-or-contingent--6e8195a096f0\\\",\n      \\\"page_refs\\\": [\n        \\\"p.474\\\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \\\"evidence_id\\\": \\\"evidence-counterexample-repair-test--e15f2c8a7d64\\\",\n      \\\"title\\\": \\\"Counterexample Repair Test\\\",\n      \\\"excerpt\\\": \\\"When faced with a case in which it seems that [p] is fully grounded in D yet ~☐(∧D → p), the advocate of necessity should check to see if there are additional explanatorily relevant facts P such that ☐(∧(D, P) → p). If such additional facts are available, we have reason to believe that D is a mere partial ground that was misidentified as a full ground; in such a case, [p] instead is fully grounded in D, P. Each of the three cases set out above are such that additional explanatorily relevant facts are available that meet the modal entailment condition: what are identified as enabling conditions in the first case, a totality fact ... in the second case, and the fact that Socrates and Xanthippe were married in the third case.\\\",\n      \\\"overview\\\": \\\"This is the paper’s general diagnostic for apparent failures of necessity. It narrows the target to full grounds and shows how several alleged counterexamples instead identify incomplete grounds. The test is important because it prevents ordinary explanatory relevance from being mistaken for complete metaphysical grounding.\\\",\n      \\\"evidence_type\\\": \\\"formal_argument\\\",\n      \\\"claim_refs\\\": [\n        \\\"claim-counterexamples-misidentify-grounds\\\",\n        \\\"claim-full-grounding-necessitates-grounded-proposition\\\"\n      ],\n      \\\"source_id\\\": \\\"source-grounding-necessary-or-contingent--6e8195a096f0\\\",\n      \\\"page_refs\\\": [\n        \\\"p.478\\\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \\\"evidence_id\\\": \\\"evidence-rose-trope-diagnosis--8f3b1c6d9e20\\\",\n      \\\"title\\\": \\\"Rose Trope Diagnosis\\\",\n      \\\"excerpt\\\": \\\"I agree that the fact that the rose is a particular shade of red seems explanatorily relevant to the fact that the particular redness exists. But, following Schnieder (2006a), tacking on the fact that the rose exists to this explanans seems to add nothing to the overall explanation. Since any partial ground in a grounding explanation is explanatorily relevant to the explanandum, it seems, then, that the existence of the particular redness isn’t even partially grounded in the existence of the rose. I suggest instead (as Schnieder does) that the existence of the particular redness is fully grounded in the fact that the rose is a particular shade of red.\\\",\n      \\\"overview\\\": \\\"This evidence handles a different kind of counterexample. Rather than adding missing grounds, Trogdon argues that the alleged ground is not a ground at all. The passage preserves necessity by relocating the full ground of the redness trope in a more specific fact about the rose’s shade, while treating the rose’s bare existence as explanatorily idle.\\\",\n      \\\"evidence_type\\\": \\\"formal_argument\\\",\n      \\\"claim_refs\\\": [\n        \\\"claim-rose-existence-is-nonground\\\",\n        \\\"claim-counterexamples-misidentify-grounds\\\"\n      ],\n      \\\"source_id\\\": \\\"source-grounding-necessary-or-contingent--6e8195a096f0\\\",\n      \\\"page_refs\\\": [\n        \\\"p.479\\\"\n      ]\n    }\n  ],\n  \\\"relations\\\": [\n    {\n      \\\"relation_type\\\": \\\"supports\\\",\n      \\\"src\\\": \\\"claim-significance-constrains-grounding\\\",\n      \\\"dst\\\": \\\"claim-full-grounding-necessitates-grounded-proposition\\\",\n      \\\"rationale\\\": \\\"The main necessity argument uses significance as premise (2).\\\"\n    },\n    {\n      \\\"relation_type\\\": \\\"supports\\\",\n      \\\"src\\\": \\\"claim-essence-closes-connecting-questions\\\",\n      \\\"dst\\\": \\\"claim-full-grounding-necessitates-grounded-proposition\\\",\n      \\\"rationale\\\": \\\"The main necessity argument uses essence to move from lack of cognitive significance to ∧D → p.\\\"\n    },\n    {\n      \\\"relation_type\\\": \\\"supports\\\",\n      \\\"src\\\": \\\"claim-essential-truths-ground-necessity\\\",\n      \\\"dst\\\": \\\"claim-full-grounding-necessitates-grounded-proposition\\\",\n      \\\"rationale\\\": \\\"Metaphysical necessity of essential truths supplies the final modal step.\\\"\n    },\n    {\n      \\\"relation_type\\\": \\\"motivates\\\",\n      \\\"src\\\": \\\"claim-grounding-distinction-is-explanatory\\\",\n      \\\"dst\\\": \\\"claim-full-grounding-necessitates-grounded-proposition\\\",\n      \\\"rationale\\\": \\\"Rejecting a purely modal analysis explains why a substantive argument for necessity is needed.\\\"\n    },\n    {\n      \\\"relation_type\\\": \\\"supports\\\",\n      \\\"src\\\": \\\"claim-paradigms-support-significance\\\",\n      \\\"dst\\\": \\\"claim-significance-constrains-grounding\\\",\n      \\\"rationale\\\": \\\"Paradigmatic grounding cases are offered as evidence for generalizing the epistemic constraint.\\\"\n    },\n    {\n      \\\"relation_type\\\": \\\"defends_against_objection\\\",\n      \\\"src\\\": \\\"claim-counterexamples-misidentify-grounds\\\",\n      \\\"dst\\\": \\\"claim-full-grounding-necessitates-grounded-proposition\\\",\n      \\\"rationale\\\": \\\"The conclusion argues several apparent failures of necessity are merely incomplete full-ground descriptions.\\\"\n    },\n    {\n      \\\"relation_type\\\": \\\"defends_against_objection\\\",\n      \\\"src\\\": \\\"claim-rose-existence-is-nonground\\\",\n      \\\"dst\\\": \\\"claim-full-grounding-necessitates-grounded-proposition\\\",\n      \\\"rationale\\\": \\\"The trope example is diagnosed as a non-ground rather than a counterexample to necessity.\\\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \\\"summary\\\": \\\"Kelly Trogdon argues that full grounding is necessary in the sense that full grounds metaphysically necessitate what they ground. The argument rests on an epistemic constraint on grounding, a Finean account of essence, and a diagnosis of apparent counterexamples as incomplete grounds or non-grounds.\\\",\n  \\\"warnings\\\": [\n    \\\"The source record reported no resolved publication date; the source text visibly indicates Pacific Philosophical Quarterly 94 (2013), but no exact date was inferred.\\\",\n    \\\"The claims and relations context directories were empty, so claims and relations were generated from the source markdown and task context only.\\\",\n    \\\"The source extraction metadata marked the pdftotext markdown as usable with no warnings, so the binary PDF was not consulted.\\\"\n  ],\n  \\\"source_dossier\\\": {\n    \\\"summary_short\\\": \\\"Kelly Trogdon argues that full grounding is necessary: full grounds metaphysically entail what they ground. The explanation combines cognitively insignificant connecting questions, Finean essence, and diagnoses of counterexamples as partial grounds or non-grounds.\\\",\n    \\\"summary_long\\\": \\\"This article addresses whether grounding is a necessary relation, where the target thesis is not that the grounding relation itself repeats in every possible world, but that full grounds necessitate what they ground: if [p] is fully grounded in D, then □(∧D → p). Trogdon begins by rejecting two easier routes. Grounding cannot inherit necessity merely from being a determination relation, since causation is also a determination relation and need not necessitate in the relevant sense. Nor can the distinction between full and partial grounding simply be defined modally, because necessary truths can have merely partial grounds while still being entailed as a trivial modal matter. This sets up the need for a substantive explanation of grounding necessity. The core argument has two premises. The significance principle says that full grounding obtains only when the connecting question, ‘Why should [p] obtain given D?’, lacks cognitive significance. The essence principle says that when such a question lacks cognitive significance, the relevant facts or entities have Fine-style essential truths according to which ∧D → p. Since essential truths are metaphysically necessary, those grounds necessitate the grounded proposition. Much of the article clarifies cognitive significance through examples involving shapes, fragility, phenomenal consciousness, and moral rightness. Questions about fragility and circles appear answerable once one grasps the relevant natures; questions about phenomenal character or moral rightness seem to retain substantive force. Trogdon uses this contrast to motivate an epistemic constraint on grounding while denying that it makes grounding subjective, because the relevant facts concern idealized rational subjects and the natures of entities, not the psychology of a particular inquirer. The final section tests the view against counterexamples. Promise-based moral obligation, universal generalizations, and widowhood cases are said to omit enabling, totality, or marriage facts that are part of the full ground. A trope case involving a rose and its redness is diagnosed differently: the rose’s bare existence is not even a partial ground of the particular redness; the specific fact that the rose is a shade of red does the grounding work. The resulting picture makes grounding necessary because full metaphysical explanation closes the relevant explanatory question through essence.\\\",\n    \\\"sections\\\": [\n      {\n        \\\"heading\\\": \\\"Central Necessity Thesis\\\",\n        \\\"body\\\": \\\"The paper’s target is a precise necessity thesis for full grounding: if [p] is fully grounded in D, then □(∧D → p). This is weaker than saying the grounding relation itself necessarily recurs, but strong enough to require that full grounds rule out worlds in which the grounded proposition fails. The claim gives grounding a modal consequence without reducing grounding to modality.\\\",\n        \\\"grounding_claim_ids\\\": [\n          \\\"claim-full-grounding-necessitates-grounded-proposition\\\"\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \\\"heading\\\": \\\"Why Modal Shortcuts Fail\\\",\n        \\\"body\\\": \\\"Trogdon rejects the idea that necessity follows simply from grounding being a determination relation or from defining full grounding as modal entailment. Necessary propositions can be partially grounded and still be trivially entailed in all worlds, so modal entailment alone cannot mark the full/partial distinction. This motivates a deeper explanatory account of why full grounds necessitate.\\\",\n        \\\"grounding_claim_ids\\\": [\n          \\\"claim-grounding-distinction-is-explanatory\\\"\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \\\"heading\\\": \\\"Cognitive Significance Constraint\\\",\n        \\\"body\\\": \\\"The significance principle gives grounding an epistemic constraint: a full grounding claim requires the associated connecting question to lack cognitive significance. The point is not that actual agents already know the explanation, but that a properly informed rational grasp of the relevant facts and entities can make the question lose substantive force. Paradigm logical grounding cases are used to support the generalization.\\\",\n        \\\"grounding_claim_ids\\\": [\n          \\\"claim-significance-constrains-grounding\\\",\n          \\\"claim-cognitive-significance-requires-no-accessible-answer\\\",\n          \\\"claim-paradigms-support-significance\\\"\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \\\"heading\\\": \\\"Essence Provides Closure\\\",\n        \\\"body\\\": \\\"The essence principle explains why some connecting questions close. On a Finean conception, real definitions disclose what entities are, and essential truths do not depend on how the world contingently turns out. If the relevant essence includes ∧D → p, then grasping that essence explains why the connecting question lacks cognitive significance and why the entailment is metaphysically necessary.\\\",\n        \\\"grounding_claim_ids\\\": [\n          \\\"claim-essence-closes-connecting-questions\\\",\n          \\\"claim-essential-truths-ground-necessity\\\"\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \\\"heading\\\": \\\"Handling Apparent Counterexamples\\\",\n        \\\"body\\\": \\\"The conclusion argues that promise, universal generalization, and widowhood examples do not defeat necessity. In each case, what looked like a full ground omits explanatorily relevant material: enabling conditions for obligation, a totality fact for universal generalization, or the marriage fact for widowhood. Once those facts are included, the full ground again meets the modal entailment condition.\\\",\n        \\\"grounding_claim_ids\\\": [\n          \\\"claim-counterexamples-misidentify-grounds\\\",\n          \\\"claim-full-grounding-necessitates-grounded-proposition\\\"\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \\\"heading\\\": \\\"Practical Interpretation\\\",\n        \\\"body\\\": \\\"For readers using grounding to analyze truthmaking, reduction, or metaphysical explanation, the paper warns against treating plausible explanatory relevance as sufficient for full grounding. A candidate ground must be checked for omitted enabling or totality facts, and some alleged grounds may be explanatorily idle. The view therefore tightens what counts as a complete metaphysical explanation.\\\",\n        \\\"grounding_claim_ids\\\": [\n          \\\"claim-counterexamples-misidentify-grounds\\\",\n          \\\"claim-rose-existence-is-nonground\\\",\n          \\\"claim-grounding-distinction-is-explanatory\\\"\n        ]\n      }\n    ]\n  }\n}\n\nwith output_path.open('w', encoding='utf-8') as f:\n    json.dump(data, f, ensure_ascii=False, indent=2)\n    f.write('\\\\n')\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
